<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Salghurid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1148 CE – 1282 CE (approx.) · capital Shiraz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Turkmen atabegs of Fars whose submission to the Mongols spared Shiraz, and whose patronage is remembered in the works of the poet Saadi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salghurids">Salghurids</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salur">Salghur</a> tribe who entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. In 1148 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunqur-ibn-mawdud">Sunqur ibn Mawdud</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> from Seljuk officials and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> as atabeg, formally a guardian for Seljuk princes but in practice independent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The atabegs ruled a prosperous province of farmland, towns and trade routes to the Gulf. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sad-i-ibn-zangi">Sa'd I ibn Zangi</a> (1198 to 1226) built walls and mosques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-sad">Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd</a> submitted to the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> Ögedei, paying tribute and keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> from destruction. Saadi took his pen name from Abu Bakr's son Sa'd and dedicated the Bustan (1257) and Gulistan (1258) to the dynasty.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Abu Bakr's death in 1260 a series of short reigns followed. The last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abish-khatun">Abish Khatun</a>, was married to the Mongol prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke-temur-son-of-hulagu">Mengü-Temür</a>, and in the 1280s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> passed under direct control of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/salghurid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Salghurid dynasty" width="320" height="276"><figcaption>Image: Demis MAP server · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Salghurids_in_1180_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1148 CE – 1282 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shiraz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sa'd I ibn Zangi, Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd, Abish Khatun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Salghurids, Atabegs of Fars</dd></dl></aside></div>

